{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey rent control framework (municipal authority)","requirement":"New Jersey has no statewide law that establishes, governs or controls rents; a landlord is subject to rent control or rent leveling only where the municipality has adopted an ordinance, enforced by a local board.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rentals in New Jersey; whether a unit is rent controlled depends on whether the tenant's municipality has adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may be exempt from local rent control; HUD-owned or -subsidized developments and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling. Locally created boards enforce these ordinances.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings exemption from local rent control","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; the exemption runs for 30 years from completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase notice and termination requirements (Anti-Eviction Act)","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease: the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered at the increased rent, with written notice that the tenant may remain at the increased rent. An increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; an increase must also comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances where they exist.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No rent increase for condominium or cooperative conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, a landlord may not increase rents to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a dwelling unit used as a residence only with the tenant's consent or through eviction, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause proved in court.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; a Notice to Quit is required for good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent, and a Notice to Cease may also be required.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New owner's responsibility for security deposits","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving notices, paying interest and returning it plus accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Security deposits for residential rental units that are sold or transferred; deposit disputes up to $5,000 go to Small Claims and those over $5,000 up to $15,000 to the Special Civil Part.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for withholding or misusing security deposit monies","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or for a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, may be penalized; unlawfully using deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Security deposits made by or on behalf of tenants receiving state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance; misuse of deposit monies by any person.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenants receiving government financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceeding, with amounts going to the state entity that made the deposits; unlawful use of deposit monies may bring a disorderly persons charge, a fine of at least $200, up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing because of a person's source of lawful income, and must provide equal treatment in the rental of housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, such as YMCA and age-restricted housing, as to familial status.","penalty":"Up to $10,000 for a first offense; a discriminating landlord may also have to pay monetary damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
